{"t": 467.22, "kind": "proposal", "iteration": 2, "k": 1, "move": "new_topology", "name": "Half-Bridge-SSHI", "rationale": "The previous Parallel-SSHI-Bridge used a full-bridge rectifier, which incurs two diode drops. Given the low open-circuit voltage (~3.2V), these drops are significant and likely caused the output to settle below 1.8V on several conditions (S1, S2, S4). By using a half-bridge rectifier (tying pzn to ground) combined with a synchronous flip at both voltage extrema (SSHI), we reduce the diode drop to a single DSCH diode. This increase in efficiency, paired with a smaller storage capacitor to improve startup time and settlement (avoiding the long time constants seen in the reference FBR), should allow the circuit to meet the vout_min and startup requirements across all scenarios while the Zener clamp handles the strong excitation of S3.", "circuit": {"move": "new_topology", "name": "Half-Bridge-SSHI", "rationale": "The previous Parallel-SSHI-Bridge used a full-bridge rectifier, which incurs two diode drops. Given the low open-circuit voltage (~3.2V), these drops are significant and likely caused the output to settle below 1.8V on several conditions (S1, S2, S4). By using a half-bridge rectifier (tying pzn to ground) combined with a synchronous flip at both voltage extrema (SSHI), we reduce the diode drop to a single DSCH diode. This increase in efficiency, paired with a smaller storage capacitor to improve startup time and settlement (avoiding the long time constants seen in the reference FBR), should allow the circuit to meet the vout_min and startup requirements across all scenarios while the Zener clamp handles the strong excitation of S3.", "done": false, "components": [{"id": "D1", "type": "D", "nodes": ["pzp", "out"], "model": "DSCH"}, {"id": "L1", "type": "L", "nodes": ["pzp", "n1"], "value": 0.01}, {"id": "S1", "type": "S", "nodes": ["n1", "pzn"], "ctrl": "G1"}, {"id": "G1", "type": "SYNC", "ton": 5e-05, "phase": 0.0, "edges": "both"}, {"id"